    <h2>Why Huntsville Contractors Need Different Marketing</h2>
    
    <p>Huntsville isn't a typical Alabama market. Between <a href="https://home.army.mil/redstone/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redstone Arsenal</a>, <a href="https://www.cummingsresearchpark.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cummings Research Park</a>, the new Mazda Toyota plant up in Limestone County, and steady aerospace and defense growth, this city has been adding population faster than almost anywhere in the South for the past decade. The Census numbers tell one story — north of 225,000 inside city limits, with the metro pushing past 500,000 — but the story underneath the numbers is what matters for contractors: a lot of those new residents have real budgets for home services.</p>

    <p>That should be good news for plumbers, HVAC contractors, roofers, painters, and landscapers in Huntsville. In a lot of ways, it is. There's more demand for skilled trades work here than in most Alabama markets.</p>

    <p>The problem is everyone else figured this out too. Walk through search results for "plumber Huntsville AL" or "HVAC repair Huntsville" and you'll find the same pattern: the top of the page is dominated by Google Ads bought by national lead-generation services, then by <a href="/articles/angi-leads/">Angi listings</a>, then by Yelp, then by a handful of contractors with serious marketing budgets. The local guy doing great work out of his shop in Hampton Cove? He's nowhere. Page three. Page four. Invisible.</p>

    <p>That's the gap we exist to close.</p>

    <h2>What We Actually Do</h2>

    <p><a href="/">Sites On Call</a> builds websites for contractors in Huntsville and the surrounding North Alabama area. The website itself is free — no upfront cost. If you want us to keep adding content to it month after month so it ranks higher in Google searches over time, that's where we charge. Plans start at $149/month. No contracts. Cancel anytime.</p>

    <p>The websites we build aren't templates with your name swapped in. Each one is built around the specific services you offer in the specific cities you serve. For Huntsville plumbers, that usually means dedicated pages for water heater installation, drain cleaning, sewer line repair, slab leak detection, and emergency plumbing — each with location-specific content for Huntsville and the neighborhoods around it (Hampton Cove, <a href="/locations/madison/">Madison</a>, Big Cove, Jones Valley, Monte Sano, etc.). For HVAC contractors, it's AC repair, AC installation, heating repair, heating installation, heat pump service, and mini-split systems. For roofers, it's residential roof replacement, storm damage assessment, gutter installation, and metal roofing.</p>

    <p>The point is depth. A one-page website that says "I'm a plumber, call me" doesn't rank in Huntsville. Not anymore. There's too much competition for that approach to work. You need a website with 10-20 pages of substantive content about what you actually do — and that's what we build. For the why behind that, see our piece on <a href="/articles/contractor-website-design/">contractor website design</a>.</p>

    <h2>Who's Showing Up When Huntsville Customers Search</h2>

    <p>One of the first things we do for any new contractor in Huntsville is a competitive snapshot — pulling up the actual search results for the trade and location and showing the contractor where they rank vs. who's beating them. Most of the time, the answer surprises them.</p>

    <p>The companies dominating the Huntsville map pack and organic results aren't always the biggest or the best — they're the ones who invested in their online presence years ago and stuck with it. Their websites have 30+ pages. They have 200+ Google reviews. They've been building this asset since before half their competitors thought about it.</p>

    <p>The good news for contractors who are starting from behind: the strategies that worked for them still work now. They take time, but they work. The contractors who start the process in 2026 will be the ones dominating Huntsville search in 2028. The ones who wait will spend the next two years writing checks to Angi.</p>

